  "final_answer": "**No NBA trades have occurred in the past two days (Nov. 5–7, 2026).** ESPN's NBA Transactions page lists only minor roster housekeeping in that window, and the latest entry in ESPN's trade tracker is still the Sept. 22 deal sending Buddy Hield and Ryan Nembhard to Charlotte for Dorian Finney-Smith.\n\nWhat ESPN does show for the last two days:\n\n- **Thursday, Nov. 5** — Toronto Raptors signed G Kevin McCullar Jr. to a two-way contract. Cleveland Cavaliers signed F Norchad Omier to a two-way contract and waived F Riley Minix.\n- **Wednesday, Nov. 4** (just outside the window) — Oklahoma City Thunder signed F Dalen Terry for the remainder of the season.\n\nOn the news side, ESPN's NBA buzz file (last updated Nov. 6) has no trades either. The biggest item is **Kristaps Porzingis stepping away from basketball** due to health issues — notably, ESPN's Anthony Slater reports this is *not* an official retirement and the Warriors can still use his $20 million salary in a trade before the **Feb. 11 deadline**, making him a name to watch rather than a completed deal. The Nov. 4 buzz entry about Porzingis is explicitly about ESPN Analytics' **Trade Lab mock trades created by fans** (e.g., Porzingis-for-Myles-Turner with Milwaukee, Porzingis-plus-a-pick-swap to Dallas) — hypotheticals, not real transactions. Other recent headlines are game/feature coverage (Wembanyama's 52-24-8 night, Doncic's 53-point game, the 76ers' 8-1 start, updated power rankings, 2027 draft rankings) rather than deals.\n\nFor context, the most recent actual trades on ESPN's tracker are all from the offseason — Sept. 22 (Hield/Nembhard to Charlotte), Sept. 8 (Pelicans–Grizzlies four-player deal), Aug. 29 (Wolves–Jazz swap), and the summer blockbusters headlined by Giannis Antetokounmpo to Miami and LaMelo Ball to Minnesota.",
